#Introduction
Platforms in the gaming industry have evolved drastically over many decades where things like conenience and accessibility to games have improved and changed the way people interact with and use gaming technology. However the evolution of systems and jumps to new technology to access
gaming have created ethical challenges with consumer rights, accessibility and privacy. Here we are going to look at multiple different areas where there is concern around ethical issues with the evolution of gaming,

#Digital game ownership
Digital game ownership has been a revolutionary advancement in the industry which allowed users to access games without a physical disc or cartridge 
copy through an online service. This service and the players access to the product is fully dependant on online services which creates a lot of controversy
around ownership and rights. There is also the issue with bugs that require fixing within games that can only be maintained through updates from an
online service, this does not guarantee people who rely on their physical copy permanent access to all the features that should be included. The European commission recognieses 
that certain games and online gaming services will become unusable if a game company decides to shut their servers down, this has been a big concern to customers who want lasting value from their gaming purchases.
Some digital licences cannot be verified without wifi accesss on the console, this prevents players from being able to access owned products and could be very frustrating if online services or multiplayer
is not even used in the game, an example of this is nintendos online licensing system where this applies, they have recently enough developed a workaround where digital cartridges can be loaded onto a console
using the digital license to allow offline play. Subscription services such as Xbox game pass offer users access to massive libraries of digital downloads for a fee every month,

#Cloud Gaming
